{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31","memory_73","memory_91"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The two live symptoms are activation (-6%) and onboarding completion (-4%), so the highest-leverage single slot is the funnel step that feeds both. memory_86 and memory_31 point the same direction (shorter onboarding / fewer required fields lifted completion and first-week activation), which makes simplify_onboarding the best-supported candidate. I am spending both verification pulls on exactly those two because they are the load-bearing evidence for my chosen action, and both are stated in soft summary terms: memory_86 says 'improved completion rates and time-to-value' with no effect size, retention window, or revenue figure, and memory_31 is the oldest record here (day 12) claiming a 'durable' lift that has not been re-tested in 64 days — during which the funnel has visibly degraded, so its conditions may no longer hold. I want the original records to see effect size, whether the lift was activation-only or carried through to paid conversion and renewal, and whether the two are actually independent findings or one experiment consolidated twice; if they are the same underlying test, my evidence base is half as strong as it looks.

I am deliberately not verifying memory_73. Promotional pricing is the emotionally obvious answer to a competitor's entry pricing, and it is the one memory with a hard number attached (+31% activations, +18% revenue), but the same record already carries renewal retention -12pp. Against a primary objective of sustainable long-term qualified revenue, a 12pp retention hit is disqualifying regardless of what the source record adds, so a pull there buys me nothing. Matching a price-sensitive competitor on price also risks re-anchoring the SMB tier permanently and importing exactly the churn-prone cohort we do not want.

Downstream value risk is real and I am flagging it rather than assuming it away: cutting required signup fields raises raw activation while potentially degrading lead qualification data, routing quality, and enterprise-prospect identification — so it can improve the metric I am measuring while diluting the qualified revenue I am actually optimizing for. It may also cannibalize memory_44's sales-assisted enterprise path if larger prospects get silently funneled into a thinner self-serve flow. Accordingly I will run this as a standard controlled experiment, not a broad rollout: hold out a control, keep the enterprise/demo-request path untouched, and instrument paid conversion, lead-qualification completeness, and 30-day retention as guardrails alongside completion rate. If verification shows memory_86 and memory_31 are one duplicated result, or that neither tracked revenue past activation, I will downgrade to a small guarded test and treat activation_messaging (memory_91) as the fallback, since it targets the same activation decline without touching the qualification data we collect.","confidence":68}
